
class Solution {
    public int firstMissingPositive(int[] nums) {

        int n = nums.length;   // length of the array

        // Step 1: Place every number at its correct index
        // If value = x, it should be placed at index x-1
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){

            // Condition checks:
            // nums[i] > 0 → ignore negative numbers
            // nums[i] <= n → ignore numbers greater than array size
            // nums[nums[i]-1] != nums[i] → avoid infinite swapping when duplicate exists
            while(nums[i] > 0 && nums[i] <= n && nums[nums[i]-1] != nums[i]){

                // Store the value at the correct position temporarily
                int temp = nums[nums[i]-1];

                // Place current number at its correct index (nums[i] -> nums[nums[i]-1])
                nums[nums[i]-1] = nums[i];

                // Put the previous value back at index i
                nums[i] = temp;
            }
        }

        // Step 2: After rearranging, check which index does not have correct value
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){

            // If index i does not contain i+1,
            // then i+1 is the smallest missing positive number
            if(nums[i] != i + 1){
                return i + 1;
            }
        }

        // Step 3: If all numbers 1..n are present
        // then the smallest missing positive is n+1
        return n + 1;
    }
}
Example Walkthrough

Input:

nums = [3,4,-1,1]
Step 1 (Place numbers correctly)

Correct positions:

1 → index 0
2 → index 1
3 → index 2
4 → index 3

After rearranging:

[1, -1, 3, 4]
Step 2 (Find mismatch)
index 0 → 1 ✔
index 1 → -1 ❌

So answer:

2
Key Idea (Very Important)

Each number tries to sit at its correct index:

value 1 → index 0
value 2 → index 1
value 3 → index 2
value 4 → index 3

First place numbers correctly, then first wrong index + 1 = answer.

Complexity
Type	Complexity
Time	O(n)
Space	O(1)
